1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It's all around us.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
So much a part of our lives, we forget it's there.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
But just try to survive a single day without packaging.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Virtually everything produced on Earth needs to be packaged.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
We've taken great pains to make sure that it gets up there in one piece.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
To sell it, ship it, protect it, or keep it fresh.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
High-tech companies you never knew existed invest billions

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:33,000
to design every box, bottle, and bag you encountered,

9
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:35,000
and to crank them out by the ton

10
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:37,000
to meet our insatiable demand.

11
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,000
This isn't rocket science. It's harder.

12
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:43,000
And you'll never believe how much of every dollar you spend

13
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:45,000
pays for the cost of it all.

14
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:50,000
Now, the untold story of packaging on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Packaging is the multi-billion dollar industry we all take for granted, despite the fact

16
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,260
that we rely on it every day.

17
00:01:16,261 --> 00:01:21,100
One glance down the aisle at your local grocery store says it all.

18
00:01:21,101 --> 00:01:27,000
Every item on every shelf is in a package, and the variety is endless.

19
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:32,300
There's an amazingly rich story behind the package of every product you buy.

20
00:01:32,301 --> 00:01:37,240
And you probably didn't even know you're a huge part of it.

21
00:01:37,241 --> 00:01:42,420
One dollar of every eleven you spend pays for the cost of packaging.

22
00:01:42,421 --> 00:01:52,760
And one third of everything you throw away consists of packaging materials.

23
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:57,840
Researchers at Michigan State University have long acknowledged a truth that most of us ignore.

24
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,480
Look at the blue one.

25
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:04,480
Packaging is a science, a combination of design and engineering.

26
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:12,320
Since 1952, the School of Packaging at Michigan State has set the tone and purpose for the industry.

27
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:15,820
Packaging serves a whole bunch of functions.

28
00:02:15,821 --> 00:02:17,400
One is to contain a product.

29
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:22,220
Another is to protect and preserve that product from all kinds of assaults.

30
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,400
It's got to help the consumer use the product.

31
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:30,500
It's got to be produced efficiently, and it's got to be environmentally friendly.

32
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:35,140
That's a pretty tall order for a single substance, a single entity to do.

33
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:40,880
The experts here know that no matter what the material, the same goal applies.

34
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:46,740
Produce the best container at the lowest cost using the least amount of material.

35
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:51,300
Consider that deceptively simple form of packaging.

36
00:02:51,301 --> 00:02:53,139
the plastic water bottle.

37
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:57,480
The water inside can only vary so much from brand to brand,

38
00:02:57,481 --> 00:03:02,280
so a staggering amount of money and manpower goes into the container.

39
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:09,000
According to the National Resources Defense Council, at least 90% of the cost of bottled

40
00:03:09,001 --> 00:03:15,900
water covers things other than the water itself, including packaging, marketing, and shipping.

41
00:03:15,901 --> 00:03:20,900
Americans go through 50 billion bottles of water annually.

42
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:30,900
And to design them, many of the beverage industry's major players turn to Amcor Rigid Plastics of Manchester, Michigan.

43
00:03:30,901 --> 00:03:35,900
On the surface, packaging a drink seems like a no-brainer.

44
00:03:35,901 --> 00:03:41,900
But designing the bottle is more complicated than you'd ever imagine.

45
00:03:41,901 --> 00:03:46,900
One reason why is they have to solve a vexing problem.

46
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:51,900
And the answer, oddly enough, comes straight out of your vegetable garden.

47
00:03:51,901 --> 00:03:56,900
The problem is that plastic bottles can easily deform shortly after they're filled.

48
00:03:56,901 --> 00:04:04,900
A liquid entering a bottle during the bottling process has been heated to 185 degrees Fahrenheit for pasteurization.

49
00:04:04,901 --> 00:04:10,900
As it cools, it creates a low-pressure vacuum that causes the bottle to contract.

50
00:04:10,901 --> 00:04:15,900
Amcor's solution is hiding diaphragms in the walls and base

51
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:17,900
that absorb the vacuum.

52
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:19,900
The inspiration for the design?

53
00:04:19,901 --> 00:04:22,900
The inside of a bell pepper.

54
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,900
We're actually working with the vacuum.

55
00:04:26,901 --> 00:04:30,900
That vacuum itself is helping to form this new geometry.

56
00:04:30,901 --> 00:04:32,900
And because these are new technologies and a new way of

57
00:04:32,901 --> 00:04:35,900
looking at absorbing the vacuum, it naturally is giving us forms

58
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:37,900
that we've not had before.

59
00:04:37,901 --> 00:04:42,900
When the design is complete, engineers test it in a virtual world.

60
00:04:42,901 --> 00:04:45,900
It's like crash testing a bottle in a computer.

61
00:04:45,901 --> 00:04:49,900
Everything that that bottle is going to experience in the real world, like top load when it's

62
00:04:49,901 --> 00:04:55,900
being filled or stacked, and also the vacuum or perhaps even internal pressure like in a soda bottle,

63
00:04:55,901 --> 00:04:58,900
we're going to go ahead and test all of that in the computer.

64
00:04:58,901 --> 00:05:06,900
Competition in the bottled beverage industry is fierce, and the look of the packaging can have a huge impact on consumer preferences.

65
00:05:06,901 --> 00:05:11,900
So graphic designers create virtual store shelves to show

66
00:05:11,901 --> 00:05:13,900
how the bottles will appear on display.

67
00:05:13,901 --> 00:05:18,900
Designers can also get a feel for how the bottle fits in your

68
00:05:18,901 --> 00:05:21,900
hand before it's ever made.

69
00:05:21,901 --> 00:05:26,900
We can simulate how a real hand of a person is going to grab

70
00:05:26,901 --> 00:05:27,900
this bottle.

71
00:05:27,901 --> 00:05:31,900
We have hands who can move, fingers we can move.

72
00:05:31,901 --> 00:05:35,900
We can see where the contact points on the bottle versus the

73
00:05:35,901 --> 00:05:39,124
the hand. All of our products do have to function.

74
00:05:39,125 --> 00:05:42,220
That's the most important piece of it is that it

75
00:05:42,221 --> 00:05:46,460
functions and serves that purpose to protect the product and stand up to all the conditions it'll

76
00:05:46,461 --> 00:05:51,715
go through from manufacturing to the store shelf and onto the consumer's home.

77
00:05:51,716 --> 00:05:52,860
After the virtual

78
00:05:52,861 --> 00:05:58,700
bottle satisfies Amcor's packaging brain trust, a 3D printer churns out a prototype.

79
00:05:58,701 --> 00:06:06,760
The print head makes several hundred passes over a volume of plastic powder, and in each

80
00:06:06,761 --> 00:06:13,400
pass, sprays a binding solution in the shape of one thin cross section of the bottle.

81
00:06:13,401 --> 00:06:18,220
Layer by layer, a solid form takes shape.

82
00:06:18,221 --> 00:06:25,700
And after three hours, the prototype is complete.

83
00:06:25,701 --> 00:06:29,500
Next, digital scanners analyze the bottle's dimensions

84
00:06:29,501 --> 00:06:31,640
to confirm that it measures up to the design.

85
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:34,840
We'll do dimensional verifications

86
00:06:34,841 --> 00:06:36,500
and make sure that on the manufacturing side,

87
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:38,100
we've got what we need.

88
00:06:38,101 --> 00:06:40,220
And we'll finish polishing this model up

89
00:06:40,221 --> 00:06:43,020
and send it off to the customer for final aesthetic approval.

90
00:06:43,021 --> 00:06:45,820
Once we've got final approval on the aesthetics,

91
00:06:45,821 --> 00:06:47,120
we're off to making bottles.

92
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:51,580
And those bottles are made from a plastic

93
00:06:51,581 --> 00:06:54,400
called polyethylene terephthalate,

94
00:06:54,401 --> 00:06:57,300
also known as PET.

95
00:06:57,301 --> 00:07:02,320
Light, flexible, and durable, it was patented in 1973

96
00:07:02,321 --> 00:07:06,820
and did for water what the pop-top can had done for soda.

97
00:07:06,821 --> 00:07:09,640
It turned water into a drink we could enjoy anywhere,

98
00:07:09,641 --> 00:07:10,341
anytime.

99
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:14,520
But our devotion to bottled water

100
00:07:14,521 --> 00:07:17,960
comes at a high environmental price.

101
00:07:17,961 --> 00:07:22,300
PET is made from petroleum, and more than 17 million barrels

102
00:07:22,301 --> 00:07:25,300
of oil are required to produce the 30 billion bottles

103
00:07:25,301 --> 00:07:29,380
purchased by Americans every year.

104
00:07:29,381 --> 00:07:32,900
Since PET is non-biodegradable, environmentalists

105
00:07:32,901 --> 00:07:36,180
advocate aggressive recycling efforts.

106
00:07:36,181 --> 00:07:39,020
But nearly 8 out of 10 plastic water bottles

107
00:07:39,021 --> 00:07:40,120
end up in a landfill.

108
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:45,480
Also, as consumers, we need to consider

109
00:07:45,481 --> 00:07:49,540
how packaging water contributes to its cost.

110
00:07:49,541 --> 00:07:57,580
A bottle of water costing $1.35 could be refilled with tap water every day for 10 years for less than the same price.

111
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:03,620
There's more eye-opening facts about the packaging of our food.

112
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:08,260
The demands of 21st century consumers have gone beyond preservation.

113
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:10,720
Now we require convenience.

114
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:14,060
It's not enough that containers keep food fresh.

115
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:16,200
They must now help us cook it.

116
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:23,280
One company answering the challenge is the Sealed Air Corporation,

117
00:08:23,281 --> 00:08:26,440
a global packaging innovator for 50 years.

118
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:34,220
Its product line, called Cryovac, seals, protects, and actually helps cook fresh and frozen food.

119
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:38,060
It works for both fresh and frozen applications.

120
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:40,020
It's steam-assisted cooking.

121
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:45,040
When the product is ready, you peel it open, and you can actually eat directly from the package.

122
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:48,220
Other innovations take the guesswork out of measuring.

123
00:08:48,221 --> 00:08:53,100
marinate on demand, and make novice chefs look like experts.

124
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:58,600
The Cryovac material may look like a simple plastic wrap,

125
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:00,820
but it's made from a patented formula.

126
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,120
It's comprised of different layers.

127
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:05,980
Some keep oxygen and bacteria out.

128
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:09,080
Others keep moisture and flavor in.

129
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:12,320
Others distribute microwave heat evenly.

130
00:09:12,321 --> 00:09:20,040
Cryovac is a high-tech descendant of one of packaging's milestone innovations.

131
00:09:20,041 --> 00:09:28,440
Cellophane, the ubiquitous transparent film made from plant fibers called cellulose, has

132
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:31,630
been a staple of food preservation since 1913.

133
00:09:31,631 --> 00:09:34,820
And like many great inventions, it was created

134
00:09:34,821 --> 00:09:36,582
completely by accident.

135
00:09:36,583 --> 00:09:41,640
Credit a Swiss textile engineer whose real intention was to invent

136
00:09:41,641 --> 00:09:43,248
a stain-proof tablecloth.

137
00:09:43,249 --> 00:09:47,300
He discovered that the cellulose he tried to apply to the cloth

138
00:09:47,301 --> 00:09:56,280
kept peeling off, as the clear, water-resistant, biodegradable film we know today.

139
00:09:56,281 --> 00:10:01,200
It paved the way for innovations like Cryovac, which is formed into bags that not only help

140
00:10:01,201 --> 00:10:05,880
packagers preserve our food, but also lend a helping hand to prepare it.

141
00:10:05,881 --> 00:10:11,720
The machine behind me is one of the main components of our packaging process.

142
00:10:11,721 --> 00:10:15,540
The product will go into a chamber system and in that chamber will evacuate the air

143
00:10:15,541 --> 00:10:19,580
out of the package and then we'll make a closing seal.

144
00:10:19,581 --> 00:10:25,260
With today's equipment we're able to completely automate that operation from ready to pack

145
00:10:25,261 --> 00:10:34,020
till it's ready to ship to a food processor or a consumer.

146
00:10:34,021 --> 00:10:37,440
Some packaging strives to improve our lives.

147
00:10:37,441 --> 00:10:40,460
Others have overstayed their welcome.

148
00:10:40,461 --> 00:10:44,520
This plastic nightmare is the packaging we love to hate.

149
00:10:44,521 --> 00:10:47,020
But a new kind of package is taking its place.

150
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:55,560
No country consumes more bottled water per person than Italy.

151
00:10:55,561 --> 00:10:59,740
Each Italian drinks an average of 55 gallons per year,

152
00:10:59,741 --> 00:11:03,100
nearly twice that of each American.

153
00:11:03,101 --> 00:11:05,820
Packaging will return on Modern Marvels.

154
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:11,440
We now return to packaging on Modern Marvels.

155
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:22,000
In recent years, a new packaging term has entered the American lexicon, rap rage.

156
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:29,260
Each year, 6,000 Americans wind up in emergency rooms with injuries suffered while trying to open packaging.

157
00:11:29,261 --> 00:11:40,360
The most common source of rap rage is the clamshell, that plastic monstrosity made of two rigid halves sealed together with ultrasonic welding.

158
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:48,857
There's sort of a love-hate relationship with this package because people love it because they know that they can see the product that they're buying and know that all

159
00:11:48,858 --> 00:11:49,760
the parts are inside.

160
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:52,080
And the retailers like it because it's hard to steal.

161
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:57,380
People hate it because it's very difficult to open with scissors and anyone who has tried understands what I'm talking about.

162
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:03,680
The cry of consumer protest has reached many packaging companies that are riding to our rescue.

163
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:13,540
LA's Impact Manufacturing and Winterborne Corporation offer a frustration-free alternative they call the EnviroShell.

164
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:20,240
A large portion of it isn't plastic, but paper, which makes it a breeze to open.

165
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:24,860
What we're looking at here is the machine that seals what we call a trap blister.

166
00:12:24,861 --> 00:12:29,700
Unlike the clamshell that was mostly plastic, a trapped blister is mostly paper, making

167
00:12:29,701 --> 00:12:30,700
it more sustainable.

168
00:12:30,701 --> 00:12:35,420
How it works is that the paper has a special coating that is heat activated.

169
00:12:35,421 --> 00:12:41,220
The product is put into a PET blister, placed inside the paper foldover, trapping it on

170
00:12:41,221 --> 00:12:47,960
the flanges, and then it is sent to this machine over here, where heat is passed through, activating

171
00:12:47,961 --> 00:12:53,760
the coating on the paper, sealing it together, giving us a nice, solid, sealed package.

172
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:58,860
Many Envirochelle packages are oversized,

173
00:12:58,861 --> 00:13:01,860
designed to not only protect contents,

174
00:13:01,861 --> 00:13:04,860
but also act as billboards to lure buyers.

175
00:13:04,861 --> 00:13:08,860
Making them requires not one, but three production facilities

176
00:13:08,861 --> 00:13:12,860
to produce the plastic, the paper, and the printing.

177
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:17,860
First up, the plastic.

178
00:13:17,861 --> 00:13:21,860
The process starts with a roll of film that's 20 mil thick.

179
00:13:21,861 --> 00:13:25,860
It comes through this section here and gets fed into the heater bank.

180
00:13:25,861 --> 00:13:29,660
The heater bank will heat it to approximately 185 degrees,

181
00:13:29,661 --> 00:13:32,160
making the plastic very pliable.

182
00:13:32,161 --> 00:13:34,560
It then goes into the forming section.

183
00:13:34,561 --> 00:13:38,760
There's an aluminum mold that is the shape of the package that we desire.

184
00:13:38,761 --> 00:13:40,360
The plastic is heated.

185
00:13:40,361 --> 00:13:46,160
It goes into the vacuum chamber and gets sucked down by a vacuum mounted underneath the tool

186
00:13:46,161 --> 00:13:50,160
and then cooled quickly to form it in the shape of the package.

187
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:58,860
Making the paper comes next at LA Corps, also in Los Angeles.

188
00:13:58,861 --> 00:14:02,860
Although the paper is the key to making the EnviroShell easier to open,

189
00:14:02,861 --> 00:14:05,860
it still needs to be strong enough to endure shipping.

190
00:14:05,861 --> 00:14:12,860
So the paper created here is corrugated, meaning it's folded into ridges that reinforce the material.

191
00:14:12,861 --> 00:14:19,860
LA Corps corrugates 700 miles of paper each day.

192
00:14:19,861 --> 00:14:26,060
What we see here is the medium going through the corrugating roll and coming out.

193
00:14:26,061 --> 00:14:31,460
If you'll notice, the medium is being heated with steam so that whenever it goes through

194
00:14:31,461 --> 00:14:35,760
the corrugating roll, it's more flexible and it won't tear out whenever it gets inside

195
00:14:35,761 --> 00:14:38,560
the corrugating roll.

196
00:14:38,561 --> 00:14:43,640
As the production line turns out layer after layer of corrugated paper, machines bond them

197
00:14:43,641 --> 00:14:48,180
together to make panels rigid enough to protect the products they'll contain.

198
00:14:48,181 --> 00:14:54,420
We've got the liner, medium, liner, medium, and then the final liner being applied.

199
00:14:54,421 --> 00:15:00,900
All of that is being applied with a thin layer of glue. It's going to be all

200
00:15:00,901 --> 00:15:06,360
combined right here at this point, go through the hot plate section for curing.

201
00:15:08,620 --> 00:15:15,620
From start to finish, computers monitor every aspect of the production process.

202
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:26,600
and workers perform quality control checks to make sure the final product is ready for the last step in making the EnviroShel.

203
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:36,400
That happens here at L.A.'s Fleetwood Fiber, where one of the world's most sophisticated printers applies colored inks to the corrugated paper.

204
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:43,860
It's a seven-color corrugated high-graphics printer and die cutter, all in line in one pass.

205
00:15:43,861 --> 00:15:45,860
The sheets are automatically unloaded.

206
00:15:45,861 --> 00:15:47,860
They're automatically fed into the up stacker.

207
00:15:47,861 --> 00:15:49,860
They go into the printing unit.

208
00:15:49,861 --> 00:15:54,860
The machine's capable of doing seven colors or six color and varnish.

209
00:15:54,861 --> 00:16:00,860
If I'm doing less than seven colors, I can actually go into the machine while it's printing

210
00:16:00,861 --> 00:16:03,860
and set up the next job.

211
00:16:03,861 --> 00:16:05,860
Everything prints above us.

212
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:10,860
Prints upside down.

213
00:16:10,861 --> 00:16:16,860
Each month, the enormous printer consumes up to 20,000 pounds of ink and coatings,

214
00:16:16,861 --> 00:16:19,860
formulated in Fleetwood's own ink lab.

215
00:16:19,861 --> 00:16:26,860
Any given number of combinations of inks are all created here, tested here,

216
00:16:26,861 --> 00:16:31,860
and ultimately are saved in the computer so that when we run a job out in the plant,

217
00:16:31,861 --> 00:16:35,860
we can repeat that with the consistency that our customers expect.

218
00:16:35,861 --> 00:16:42,580
The rainbow of inks created here enables the mega printer to crank out up to 10,000 sheets

219
00:16:42,581 --> 00:16:45,380
per hour.

220
00:16:45,381 --> 00:16:48,040
Then it automatically die cuts the final pieces.

221
00:16:48,041 --> 00:16:53,100
What you're looking at here is our rotary die cut die board.

222
00:16:53,101 --> 00:16:59,820
In order for the die cut to go cut the corrugated and not get stuck, we need to be rubbered up.

223
00:16:59,821 --> 00:17:02,740
So all these rubber pieces are like springs.

224
00:17:02,741 --> 00:17:07,460
comes through and it die cuts the corrugated it springs back die cuts the

225
00:17:07,461 --> 00:17:12,940
corrugated and it just does that in a rotary fashion colorful corrugated

226
00:17:12,941 --> 00:17:17,120
panels emerge from the printer ready for their transformation into retail

227
00:17:17,121 --> 00:17:24,060
packaging all that's left to do now is bring the paper and plastic components

228
00:17:24,061 --> 00:17:30,680
together and you've got a completed enviro shell move over clamshell this

229
00:17:30,681 --> 00:17:34,840
innovative package provides just as much protection without giving consumers

230
00:17:34,841 --> 00:17:41,840
trying to open it a nasty case of wrap rage. These professionals also excel at

231
00:17:41,841 --> 00:17:48,560
packaging solutions. They have to because their job is to pack and ship one of the

232
00:17:48,561 --> 00:17:51,960
world's largest cranes.

233
00:17:54,700 --> 00:17:59,120
Half of all the paper produced in the United States is used to wrap and

