327
00:24:31,801 --> 00:24:33,840
Certain aircraft have height limitations.

328
00:24:33,841 --> 00:24:37,860
Some aircraft have contour limitations, weight limitations.

329
00:24:37,861 --> 00:24:41,020
So we as cargo processors look at that daily.

330
00:24:41,021 --> 00:24:44,680
Before we build any pallet, we know what we're building it for.

331
00:24:44,681 --> 00:24:47,300
To maximize the use of cargo space,

332
00:24:47,301 --> 00:24:49,560
crews use computer-assisted lasers

333
00:24:49,561 --> 00:24:53,580
to analyze the size and shape of every pallet.

334
00:24:53,581 --> 00:24:56,420
Each pallet is labeled with a radio frequency identification

335
00:24:56,421 --> 00:25:00,560
tag so it can be tracked in transit.

336
00:25:00,561 --> 00:25:03,600
On any given day we'll have approximately 600 tons of cargo.

337
00:25:03,601 --> 00:25:05,960
That's a lot of stuff out there in the system,

338
00:25:05,961 --> 00:25:07,760
and that's a lot of customers wanting

339
00:25:07,761 --> 00:25:08,860
to know where their stuff is.

340
00:25:08,861 --> 00:25:12,180
So every single pallet will have an RFID tag.

341
00:25:12,181 --> 00:25:14,760
After a pallet completes its packaging prep,

342
00:25:14,761 --> 00:25:16,980
it awaits its airborne journey in a state-of-the-art

343
00:25:16,981 --> 00:25:18,060
computerized warehouse.

344
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,780
Cargo is organized by destination.

345
00:25:23,781 --> 00:25:27,640
A full plane load can be retrieved and ready to ship within two hours.

346
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:32,920
Just prior to departure, pallets are moved to staging docks,

347
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:36,820
where they're grouped to provide the most efficient use of space aboard the aircraft.

348
00:25:40,140 --> 00:25:44,880
Then servicemen operating vehicles called K-loaders roll into action.

349
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:50,940
A K-loader can carry up to 60,000 pounds and load six pallets at a time.

350
00:25:53,780 --> 00:26:06,260
Inside the C-5 planes, the cargo is carefully positioned, ensuring that no space is wasted

351
00:26:06,261 --> 00:26:08,600
in the 121-foot-long hold.

352
00:26:10,720 --> 00:26:16,560
The hold is wide enough to house an eight-lane bowling alley and roomy enough for six Greyhound

353
00:26:16,561 --> 00:26:17,261
buses.

354
00:26:17,262 --> 00:26:23,660
If I upload this piece of cargo that has a full-up armor kit for Humvee on Friday,

355
00:26:23,780 --> 00:26:26,980
on that next Monday, that person over in Afghanistan

356
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:28,680
is going to be putting onto his Humvee

357
00:26:28,780 --> 00:26:31,080
and drive away safely because of the pallet

358
00:26:31,180 --> 00:26:32,580
that I uploaded for him.

359
00:26:32,680 --> 00:26:35,320
We're very grateful for everything that we can do

360
00:26:35,420 --> 00:26:37,420
to help the people that are over there fighting

361
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:39,320
for us every day.

362
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:42,140
Although shipping by air is fast and efficient,

363
00:26:42,240 --> 00:26:44,240
it's also costly.

364
00:26:44,340 --> 00:26:46,840
So when the time factor is less critical,

365
00:26:46,940 --> 00:26:51,480
U.S. Transcom ships its cargo by land and sea.

366
00:26:51,481 --> 00:26:56,040
The Port of Baltimore is one of US Transcom's key packaging points for shipping cargo,

367
00:26:57,120 --> 00:26:59,760
including $30 million Chinook helicopters.

368
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:07,260
A packaging procedure once used extensively but now abandoned

369
00:27:07,261 --> 00:27:12,140
was to essentially shrink-wrap choppers to protect them during shipment.

370
00:27:15,260 --> 00:27:20,280
The shrink-wrap strategy has now been replaced by enclosing the choppers in a different type of container.

371
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:26,480
The environmentally controlled cargo hold of a Navy sea lift ship,

372
00:27:26,481 --> 00:27:30,480
like the USNS Mendoza.

373
00:27:30,481 --> 00:27:35,480
But before the choppers can be loaded, they need to be packaged for the trip.

374
00:27:35,481 --> 00:27:40,480
The most important part of that effort is removing and securing the rotor blades,

375
00:27:40,481 --> 00:27:45,480
which weigh between 350 and 600 pounds.

376
00:27:45,481 --> 00:27:48,480
It takes a lot of teamwork, a lot of communication,

377
00:27:48,481 --> 00:27:52,320
because you've got guys up top that are disconnecting things and guys down at the bottom

378
00:27:52,321 --> 00:27:56,640
they're actually holding the rotor blade in place we have a man that operates our spider crane that

379
00:27:56,641 --> 00:28:01,440
lifts the blade cradle up to the blade captures the blade holds it while we disconnect all the

380
00:28:01,441 --> 00:28:07,600
main attaching components and that's what we use to lower the blade down the container that

381
00:28:07,601 --> 00:28:14,160
protects the blades in transit is the helicopter itself the blades are very expensive they're made

382
00:28:14,161 --> 00:28:18,560
out of a fiberglass composite material and you have to be very careful when we're storing the

383
00:28:18,561 --> 00:28:24,080
blades and also with the removal at the port ops it's uh it is a high visibility high dollar operation

384
00:28:25,520 --> 00:28:29,680
after the rotor blades are secure and the rotor head is wrapped the helicopters are

385
00:28:29,681 --> 00:28:33,360
lifted by crane onto the mendonza and down into the cargo hold

386
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:39,760
only one step remains for packaging and shipping the chinook helicopters

387
00:28:39,761 --> 00:28:44,880
tying them down to make certain rough seas don't move them during transit.

388
00:28:44,881 --> 00:28:49,760
On helicopters, we don't like to use chains because there's no flex involved when the

389
00:28:49,761 --> 00:28:54,400
aircraft and or boat is moving while it's underway. It can actually cause a chain to

390
00:28:54,401 --> 00:28:58,689
actually pull out one of our tie down points. So that's why we prefer to use straps.

391
00:28:58,690 --> 00:28:59,390
It gives a

392
00:28:59,391 --> 00:29:00,678
little flex. U.S.

393
00:29:00,679 --> 00:29:07,200
Transcom cargo ships like the Mendoza not only help keep America's military

394
00:29:07,201 --> 00:29:12,700
fully equipped, but also redefine our notions of packaging.

395
00:29:12,701 --> 00:29:16,540
As I tell General Petraeus or General Mattis and the other

396
00:29:16,541 --> 00:29:19,380
combatant commanders, my job is to make sure they never worry

397
00:29:19,381 --> 00:29:22,280
about this. I will get it to them when they need it, and if I

398
00:29:22,281 --> 00:29:26,420
got to speed things up, I'll take care of that.

399
00:29:26,421 --> 00:29:29,580
Like U.S. Transcom, the average Joes face their own

400
00:29:29,581 --> 00:29:33,900
packaging challenges. And we come armed with a material that's

401
00:29:33,901 --> 00:29:40,660
It's a pop culture icon of such magnitude that it has 2 million fans on Facebook.

402
00:29:40,661 --> 00:29:50,560
But you won't believe what purpose its inventors originally intended it to serve.

403
00:29:50,561 --> 00:30:01,080
Packaging and transporting one Chinook helicopter aboard a C-5 cargo jet costs more than $500,000.

404
00:30:01,081 --> 00:30:03,540
will return on Modern Marvels.

405
00:30:09,180 --> 00:30:11,820
Nearly 10% of the price of any product

406
00:30:11,821 --> 00:30:14,040
is the cost of the packaging.

407
00:30:14,041 --> 00:30:16,120
But the big bucks you fork over for packaging

408
00:30:16,121 --> 00:30:17,960
