00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:35,400
The average American uses 60 pounds of plastic packaging material every year.

413
00:30:38,920 --> 00:30:41,320
And much of it is this iconic brand.

414
00:30:45,640 --> 00:30:50,920
Behold the marvel of bubble wrap, one of civilization's most popular packaging brands

415
00:30:51,480 --> 00:30:55,128
that spawned a company now worth four billion dollars.

416
00:30:55,129 --> 00:30:57,560
Our obsession to pop it makes it one

417
00:30:57,561 --> 00:31:00,320
one of life's leading time wasters,

418
00:31:00,321 --> 00:31:02,260
and an oddly effective stress reliever.

419
00:31:03,340 --> 00:31:05,900
It was invented in 1957,

420
00:31:05,901 --> 00:31:08,460
made of two shower curtains sealed together

421
00:31:08,461 --> 00:31:11,540
by entrepreneurs Alfred Fielding and Marc Chavons.

422
00:31:13,540 --> 00:31:16,180
They later started mass producing it on this machine.

423
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,640
But believe it or not, it was all a mistake,

424
00:31:20,641 --> 00:31:22,060
since their real goal was to make

425
00:31:22,061 --> 00:31:24,080
a funky new kind of wallpaper.

426
00:31:24,081 --> 00:31:30,680
They needed to create a textured padded wallpaper that would be laminated to wallpaper and to

427
00:31:30,681 --> 00:31:33,680
be applied to the wall that would give it a third dimension.

428
00:31:33,681 --> 00:31:39,960
The inventors quickly realized that they had an outstanding cushion material.

429
00:31:39,961 --> 00:31:45,580
Bubble wrap is now produced at sealed air production facilities around the world, including

430
00:31:45,581 --> 00:31:48,320
its birthplace in Saddlebrook, New Jersey.

431
00:31:48,321 --> 00:31:54,260
Bubble wrap actually starts from a process of various types of polyethylene resins.

432
00:31:54,261 --> 00:31:59,540
We mix these different ingredients up, up into our blender at the top up here.

433
00:31:59,541 --> 00:32:03,200
There's various types of ingredients that are then forced down into our extruder at

434
00:32:03,201 --> 00:32:04,440
the lower level.

435
00:32:04,441 --> 00:32:09,360
We actually then extrude two layers of film that one of those layers will be used to form

436
00:32:09,361 --> 00:32:10,360
the bubble.

437
00:32:10,361 --> 00:32:16,120
The machine that transforms the plastic film into bubble wrap has changed considerably

438
00:32:16,121 --> 00:32:17,640
in the past 50 years.

439
00:32:17,641 --> 00:32:22,640
As you watch it work its magic, let's debunk them now.

440
00:32:22,641 --> 00:32:25,640
Most people think we're actually blowing air into the bubble

441
00:32:25,641 --> 00:32:29,640
to form the bubble, but it's actually a heated drum with a vacuum.

442
00:32:29,641 --> 00:32:33,640
So we're sucking the molten flat film down into the embossing cylinder,

443
00:32:33,641 --> 00:32:37,640
and then the other hot molten film is actually laminated to the bubble

444
00:32:37,641 --> 00:32:41,640
to trap the air that forms the actual bubble impression.

445
00:32:41,641 --> 00:32:44,640
And then it immediately travels over to the chill roller,

446
00:32:44,641 --> 00:32:51,240
roller where it's quickly cooled and it forms the final impression as far as the bubble.

447
00:32:51,241 --> 00:32:57,240
Each bubble in bubble wrap is uniform in size, but the width and length of the rolls is determined

448
00:32:57,241 --> 00:32:59,240
by Sealed Air's clients.

449
00:32:59,241 --> 00:33:03,740
All of our bubble wrap brand products are produced to order, so it gives us the ability

450
00:33:03,741 --> 00:33:05,440
to slit and perf in line.

451
00:33:05,441 --> 00:33:09,240
And as you can see, there's no waste during our extrusion process.

452
00:33:09,241 --> 00:33:14,620
We trim off the sides, it goes into a vacuum tube underneath the floor, ends up back in

453
00:33:14,621 --> 00:33:16,720
and the extruder turns back into a resin

454
00:33:16,721 --> 00:33:18,060
so we can extrude it more product.

455
00:33:20,260 --> 00:33:23,080
The discards are melted and formed into pellets

456
00:33:23,081 --> 00:33:24,700
that become more bubble wrap.

457
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:28,060
The same goes for truckloads of used bubble wrap

458
00:33:28,061 --> 00:33:29,860
returned to the factory each week,

459
00:33:30,660 --> 00:33:34,500
part of the 32% of garbage that Americans recycle every year.

460
00:33:36,660 --> 00:33:38,440
A lot of the stuff that you see here now

461
00:33:38,441 --> 00:33:40,260
is returns that customers use it,

462
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:42,540
and when they're done with it, they bring it back to us.

463
00:33:42,820 --> 00:33:44,200
We reprocess it.

464
00:33:44,201 --> 00:33:45,960
We don't want any of this to go into the landfill.

465
00:33:47,040 --> 00:33:50,040
Each year, sealed air makes enough of this packaging wonder

466
00:33:50,041 --> 00:33:52,460
to wrap around the equator 10 times.

467
00:33:54,380 --> 00:33:57,640
Being mostly air, the huge rolls are light in weight.

468
00:33:58,980 --> 00:34:02,620
But the volume of space required to ship and store them is huge.

469
00:34:03,820 --> 00:34:06,440
The answer to this problem is a new type of bubble wrap

470
00:34:06,441 --> 00:34:08,440
called New Air IB.

471
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:11,680
The IB stands for inflatable bubble.

472
00:34:11,681 --> 00:34:13,680
It's our fastest-grown product line.

473
00:34:13,681 --> 00:34:17,680
It's a roll of film that the customer blows up at their own factory.

474
00:34:17,681 --> 00:34:20,680
This 24 rolls equates to a full truckload of bubbles,

475
00:34:20,681 --> 00:34:22,680
which is 70 to 80 bundles of bubbles.

476
00:34:22,681 --> 00:34:24,680
So it's a real space saver for the customer.

477
00:34:27,680 --> 00:34:32,680
This machine takes on the challenge of inflating the hundreds of thousands of bubbles on a single roll.

478
00:34:34,680 --> 00:34:37,680
That doesn't happen until the company doing the packaging is ready to ship.

479
00:34:37,681 --> 00:34:38,621
ready to ship.

480
00:34:38,622 --> 00:34:44,000
There's one key difference between this inflatable bubble wrap and the original.

481
00:34:46,160 --> 00:34:51,318
All the air pockets on a roll are interconnected, making it impossible to pop.

482
00:34:51,319 --> 00:34:52,640
So if you're looking

483
00:34:52,641 --> 00:34:55,520
for stress relief, stick with the original.

484
00:34:55,521 --> 00:34:58,400
But what about packaging fragile items that

485
00:34:58,401 --> 00:35:00,720
need more than a cushion of air to survive shipping?

486
00:35:00,721 --> 00:35:08,300
Sealed air offers foam, but not just any foam.

487
00:35:08,301 --> 00:35:13,700
It's called Instapack, and it's the result of two separate liquid chemicals that expand

488
00:35:13,701 --> 00:35:18,680
enormously when they're combined.

489
00:35:18,681 --> 00:35:23,220
The process can be performed at shipping sites, or even in your home.

490
00:35:23,221 --> 00:35:32,500
Easily activated by simply breaking a seal and mixing the two components back and forth

491
00:35:32,501 --> 00:35:35,220
until the liquid foam starts to rise.

492
00:35:35,221 --> 00:35:47,780
The bag is then placed into the box and the product is packaged for shipment.

493
00:35:47,781 --> 00:35:52,500
No matter how effective your package's cushioning material is, you still need a sturdy container

494
00:35:52,501 --> 00:35:53,730
to hold it all.

495
00:35:53,731 --> 00:35:59,880
Enter that indispensable icon of the packaging industry, the cardboard box.

496
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:07,680
Cardboard was invented by the Chinese in the 16th century, but it wasn't until 1879 that a

497
00:36:07,681 --> 00:36:12,680
Brooklyn bag maker named Robert Gare had the ingenious idea that turned it into today's

498
00:36:12,681 --> 00:36:15,732
mass-produced packaging marvel.

499
00:36:15,733 --> 00:36:21,540
A die-cut and scored box that could be stored flat and then

500
00:36:21,541 --> 00:36:23,550
easily folded for use.

501
00:36:23,551 --> 00:36:29,580
Without it, we'd still be shipping most products in wooden crates,

502
00:36:29,581 --> 00:36:38,100
and there'd be no cardboard box industry worth $50 billion.

503
00:36:38,101 --> 00:36:43,961
Packaging this high-tech wonder had the experts at NASA scratching their heads.

504
00:36:43,962 --> 00:36:45,000
Their solution

505
00:36:45,001 --> 00:36:46,760
was truly out of this world.

506
00:36:52,360 --> 00:36:56,640
The Sealed Air Corporation's first major client was IBM,

507
00:36:56,641 --> 00:37:00,720
which used bubble wrap to ship its IBM 1401, one

508
00:37:00,721 --> 00:37:02,940
of the world's first mass-produced business

509
00:37:02,941 --> 00:37:04,640
computers.

510
00:37:04,641 --> 00:37:07,300
Packaging will return on Modern Marvels.

511
00:37:10,120 --> 00:37:12,960
We now return to packaging on Modern Marvels.

512
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:23,880
Packaging can play a crucial role in settings you've probably never conceived of, like disaster

513
00:37:23,881 --> 00:37:25,880
sites.

514
00:37:25,881 --> 00:37:30,600
Imagine how handy it would be to have a giant impermeable bag that could store oil-slicked

515
00:37:30,601 --> 00:37:40,500
debris in the wake of a major spill, or sand to create sandbags against rising floodwaters.

516
00:37:40,501 --> 00:37:45,620
The PacTech Corporation of Clinton, Louisiana, has answered the challenge.

517
00:37:45,621 --> 00:37:51,260
Their mega bags can even safely package radioactive waste and have one key advantage over the

518
00:37:51,261 --> 00:37:55,540
steel containers used extensively for decades.

519
00:37:55,541 --> 00:38:01,140
The benefits of flexible packaging over the standard metal containers is the space required

520
00:38:01,141 --> 00:38:02,140
to store it.

521
00:38:02,141 --> 00:38:08,000
Typically, these projects are very large and the number of containers needed are large.

522
00:38:08,001 --> 00:38:11,060
With a flexible container, we can package tons of units

523
00:38:11,061 --> 00:38:12,060
on pallets.

524
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:17,940
But how do you make a bag for a radioactive waste

525
00:38:17,941 --> 00:38:22,220
that's just as effective as a rigid steel drum?

526
00:38:22,221 --> 00:38:25,680
The secret lies in multiple layers of heavy plastic

527
00:38:25,681 --> 00:38:27,320
and woven fiber materials.

528
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:31,320
We have two main materials.

529
00:38:31,321 --> 00:38:33,780
This is a six-ounce woven polypropylene.

530
00:38:33,781 --> 00:38:35,660
And we also have the felt material.

531
00:38:35,661 --> 00:38:36,980
It's a non-woven.

532
00:38:36,981 --> 00:38:37,860
It's a 12-ounce.

533
00:38:37,861 --> 00:38:38,776
It's pretty thick.

534
00:38:38,777 --> 00:38:42,540
Whatever going into the bag won't puncture through that 12-ounce material.

535
00:38:44,760 --> 00:38:48,880
But it's not just the heavy-duty material that makes the bag such effective packages.

536
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:55,540
It's also the heavy thread, used to stitch the layers together.

537
00:38:57,440 --> 00:39:01,320
Consumer thread is graded at a thickness between 100 to 200 denier,

538
00:39:02,080 --> 00:39:04,400
the unit of measurement for the density of fibers.

539
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:07,660
PacTech thread is 30 times heavier.

540
00:39:07,860 --> 00:39:12,860
And the sewing machine stitching it is truly industrial strength.

541
00:39:12,861 --> 00:39:18,860
Don't get your hands near it because it'll sew anything that goes through it.

542
00:39:18,861 --> 00:39:25,860
Each bag comes equipped with straps and zippers that make moving and using it easier.

543
00:39:25,861 --> 00:39:29,860
Construction methods ensure that these features won't leak or rupture.

544
00:39:29,861 --> 00:39:36,860
These straps are actually what is used to lift this bag up, full of debris, dirt, whatever they're putting into it.

545
00:39:36,861 --> 00:39:40,019
into it. Once they get it, they will fill it up and they'll zip it.

546
00:39:40,020 --> 00:39:40,820
Sometimes they'll

547
00:39:40,821 --> 00:39:44,958
put dirt, sometimes they'll put crushed concrete.

548
00:39:44,959 --> 00:39:48,420
There's no telling what they put in them.

549
00:39:48,421 --> 00:39:53,900
To make sure the bags are hefty enough to handle radioactive waste, Pac-Tec runs tests

550
00:39:53,901 --> 00:39:55,784
at its facility.

551
00:39:55,785 --> 00:40:04,260
What they do is typically load those with heavy equipment into the bags,

552
00:40:04,261 --> 00:40:09,440
the bag out of the loading frame that it's sitting in and load them onto flight bed trucks

553
00:40:09,441 --> 00:40:14,780
or rail cars to be transported to disposal sites that are approved for low-level radioactive

554
00:40:14,781 --> 00:40:17,780
soils and construction debris.

555
00:40:17,781 --> 00:40:22,900
As packaging experts are solving environmental problems on Earth, they're also pitching

556
00:40:22,901 --> 00:40:25,740
in as we reach further into space.

557
00:40:25,741 --> 00:40:33,440
In 2010, engineers at NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston faced a unique challenge.

558
00:40:33,441 --> 00:40:38,840
They had to package a complex $10 million human-like robot for a rough ride into orbit

559
00:40:38,841 --> 00:40:44,420
on the space shuttle.

560
00:40:44,421 --> 00:40:50,440
Robonaut 2, R2 for short, was designed to help astronauts by performing routine menial

561
00:40:50,441 --> 00:40:54,620
tasks on the International Space Station.

562
00:40:54,621 --> 00:40:57,820
But first he had to get there, and safely.

563
00:40:57,821 --> 00:41:02,680
What we're most concerned about during launch is the vibration environment.

564
00:41:02,681 --> 00:41:06,600
We've all seen images of the crew in the cockpit of the shuttle during launch.

565
00:41:06,601 --> 00:41:08,320
They get buffeted about quite a bit.

566
00:41:08,321 --> 00:41:10,240
It's a very dynamic environment.

567
00:41:10,241 --> 00:41:15,040
So it's something that we have to design for and take care of during that ascent period

568
00:41:15,041 --> 00:41:23,160
of launch.

569
00:41:23,161 --> 00:41:28,680
Since Robonaut 2 weighs a hefty 330 pounds, you might think he's sturdy enough to survive

570
00:41:28,681 --> 00:41:31,520
launch without much help.

571
00:41:31,521 --> 00:41:36,360
But he's also very fragile, particularly in his extremities.

572
00:41:36,361 --> 00:41:39,700
R2's hands have been produced with unprecedented dexterity.

573
00:41:39,701 --> 00:41:45,360
The sensing capabilities it has at the fingertips can detect on the order of grams, so you can

574
00:41:45,361 --> 00:41:50,980
literally put a quarter on its finger and it would know that the quarter's there.

575
00:41:50,981 --> 00:41:56,100
Could NASA's packaging experts preserve R2's fragile anatomy during the critical launch

576
00:41:56,101 --> 00:41:58,560
phase?

577
00:41:58,561 --> 00:42:03,800
Initial efforts were disappointing, including one that bundled R2 in a foam cushion container

578
00:42:03,801 --> 00:42:06,140
that failed to provide enough protection.

579
00:42:09,240 --> 00:42:13,400
The eventual solution came in the form of a box dubbed Sleeper,

580
00:42:13,960 --> 00:42:18,620
short for Structural Launch Enclosure to Effectively Protect Robonaut.

581
00:42:20,200 --> 00:42:23,100
NASA chronicled its construction with a time-lapse camera.

582
00:42:23,700 --> 00:42:26,820
What they have on the table at the moment is the base plate of our Sleeper box.

583
00:42:26,821 --> 00:42:29,820
We start out with just that one plate.

584
00:42:29,821 --> 00:42:33,820
Struts were then positioned to support R2's back and shoulders.

585
00:42:33,821 --> 00:42:35,820
At this point, he's fully attached and self-supported.

586
00:42:35,821 --> 00:42:37,820
They attach the brackets to Robonaut.

587
00:42:37,821 --> 00:42:39,820
That's the first step after he attaches to the base plate.

588
00:42:39,821 --> 00:42:41,820
The next thing they do is they put on the rear panel.

589
00:42:45,820 --> 00:42:49,820
Dense foam provided more support, followed by an aluminum frame.

590
00:42:49,821 --> 00:42:52,820
Now you have a completely enclosed sleeper.

591
00:42:52,821 --> 00:42:54,820
It's got foam inside. It's got foam outside.

592
00:42:54,821 --> 00:42:57,160
It's got Velcro straps to hold the foam together.

593
00:42:57,161 --> 00:43:01,280
So he is completely now enclosed and protected.

594
00:43:01,281 --> 00:43:04,460
Since R2 is almost human, it's only fitting

595
00:43:04,461 --> 00:43:08,260
that his position during launch will be flat on his back,

596
00:43:08,261 --> 00:43:11,080
just like the astronauts on the flight deck.

597
00:43:11,081 --> 00:43:13,600
And if you're wondering what this prized package can do

598
00:43:13,601 --> 00:43:18,140
aboard the International Space Station, here's a sample.

599
00:43:18,141 --> 00:43:20,620
I'm going to show you a case where the robot is working

600
00:43:20,621 --> 00:43:21,880
with thermal blankets.

601
00:43:21,881 --> 00:43:23,560
To be able to work with something like this,

602
00:43:23,561 --> 00:43:24,680
you have to have the flexibility.

603
00:43:24,681 --> 00:43:27,640
with, in this case, human-like hands.

604
00:43:27,740 --> 00:43:29,020
After pulling back the space blanket,

605
00:43:29,120 --> 00:43:30,920
which is something it would do to help the crew

606
00:43:31,020 --> 00:43:32,420
in terms of setting up a work site,

607
00:43:32,520 --> 00:43:34,120
we're also going to work with another flexible item,

608
00:43:34,220 --> 00:43:35,920
something you don't normally see robots working with.

609
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:37,320
In this case, it's an envelope, and it's

610
00:43:37,420 --> 00:43:38,320
going to hand it to me.

611
00:43:41,600 --> 00:43:44,140
Thank you, Robonaut.

612
00:43:44,240 --> 00:43:46,100
Chalk up the packing of Robonaut

613
00:43:46,200 --> 00:43:49,080
among packaging's countless successes.

614
00:43:49,180 --> 00:43:53,640
And to borrow a phrase from that world, as well as Hollywood's,

615
00:43:53,641 --> 00:43:55,640
That's a wrap.

